In his films, Chaplin raises his thick black eyebrow(眉毛). He straightens his coat or swings his walking stick in the air. He hides behind a fat lady or climbs under a table to escape from his enemies. Trying to be brave, he faints away on the floor. He pretend to be what he is not and never could be a rich, successful, important man. It is the secret of Chaplin’s huge success.

He discovered the character of the little tramp almost by accident. As young men, he and his brother travelled to American in a small company of actors and acted in various cities. One day Charles was invited to join a new company that as making comedies. It was in his second film that he wore the clothes that made his reputation: black hat, tight coat, big trousers, huge shoes, moustache(胡须)and walking stick. He intended simply to make people laugh. But the odd disguises(扮相) made him look comic and sad.

His appearance was a popular success, right from the beginning. But his early films hadn’t much story. They were full of actions. The little man played fast-moving games of hide-and –seek with his enemies, racing down city streets, jumping on trains, and boating down rivers.

